Amazon CloudFront Service Notes
Amazon CloudFront is a fast content delivery network (CDN) service that securely delivers data, videos, applications, and APIs to customers globally with low latency and high transfer speeds.
1. Core Concepts & Terminology
•	CDN (Content Delivery Network): A geographically distributed network of proxy servers and data centers.
•	Edge Location (POP - Point of Presence): The global data centers where CloudFront caches copies of your content. When a user requests content, they are routed to the nearest edge location.
•	Origin: The source of the content that the CDN distributes. This can be an Amazon S3 bucket, an EC2 instance, an Elastic Load Balancer, or any external custom HTTP server.
•	Distribution: The primary CloudFront configuration entity. It manages how content is delivered from your origins to your users.
•	Cache Hit Ratio: The percentage of requests that are served directly from the edge cache, which improves performance and reduces load on your origin server.
2. Key Features and Operation
How CloudFront Works:
1.	A user requests a file (e.g., image.jpg) from your website.
2.	The DNS routes the request to the nearest CloudFront edge location.
3.	The edge location checks its cache for the file.
4.	If cached (Cache Hit): The file is immediately returned to the user (low latency).
5.	If not cached (Cache Miss): The edge location retrieves the file from the Origin (e.g., S3 or EC2).
6.	The file is simultaneously returned to the user and cached at the edge location for future requests.
Caching and TTL (Time To Live):
•	Default TTL: CloudFront typically uses the origin's cache-control headers. If none are present, the default is 24 hours.
•	Invalidation: You can manually clear cached content across all edge locations before the TTL expires if you need to update a file immediately (e.g., deploying a new logo).
3. Security and Optimization
•	HTTPS Support: CloudFront supports end-to-end encryption (Viewer Protocol Policy and Origin Protocol Policy).
•	OAI (Origin Access Identity) / OAC (Origin Access Control): A virtual user identity used to securely restrict access to an S3 bucket. This prevents users from bypassing CloudFront and accessing your S3 content directly via the S3 URL. OAC is the modern, preferred method.
•	AWS WAF (Web Application Firewall): You can integrate WAF with CloudFront distributions to protect against common web exploits (like SQL injection or XSS attacks).
•	Geo-Restriction: Allows you to block users from specific geographic locations from accessing your content.
4. Price Classes
CloudFront uses a global network architecture and prices based on the geographic region where the data is served. You can optimize costs by selecting a price class:
•	Price Class All (Recommended): Uses all global edge locations for the best performance.
•	Price Class 200: Uses the majority of locations (excl. some latency-sensitive regions like Australia, South America, Asia regions) for lower cost.
•	Price Class 100: Uses only the most cost-effective North American and European locations.
5. Integration with Route 53
CloudFront is commonly used with Route 53:
•	A Route 53 Alias Record maps your domain name (e.g., www.example.com) to your CloudFront distribution domain name (e.g., d1234abcd.cloudfront.net).


